  w1_s2_off    wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s2          128x256x64 s2 cluster 1x1x1 mc -, 384 thr, 32p/232c regs | 2 stages x 49152 B + 32 B barriers = 98336 B of 232448 B (42.3%)
      why Depth 2 without the cluster -- the shallow end, and the row that says how much of the deficit is fill latency at all
  w1_s2_mcb2   wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s2_mcb2     128x256x64 s2 cluster 1x2x1 mc B, 384 thr, 32p/232c regs | 2 stages x 49152 B + 32 B barriers = 98336 B of 232448 B (42.3%)
      why Depth 2 with the B cluster. Pairing every measurable depth at all three cluster settings is what keeps 'deeper is better' and 'this cluster is better' from being one measurement
  w1_s2_mc2    wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s2_mc2      128x256x64 s2 cluster 2x1x1 mc A, 384 thr, 32p/232c regs | 2 stages x 49152 B + 32 B barriers = 98336 B of 232448 B (42.3%)
      why Depth 2 with the A cluster -- the shallow end of the control axis, and round 2's worst 128x256 row (57.7% at sq4096), which is the shape of a ring too short to hide the fill it still pays for
  w1_s5_mc2    wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s5_mc2      DECLINED | 5 stages x 49152 B + 80 B barriers = 245840 B of 232448 B (105.8%)
      ptx_wgmma::UNSUPPORTED: wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s5_mc2: 245840 B of shared memory (5 stages x 49152 B + 80 B of barriers) exceeds the 232448 B per-CTA ceiling
      why Depth 5 at 128x256. EXPECTED TO DECLINE on shared memory -- and the decline is the point: no cluster changes the ring's size, whichever operand it multicasts, so the depth axis stops at 4 for this tile at ALL THREE cluster settings
  w1_s6_mc2    wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s6_mc2      DECLINED | 6 stages x 49152 B + 96 B barriers = 295008 B of 232448 B (126.9%)
      ptx_wgmma::UNSUPPORTED: wgmma_nt_f16_128x256x64_s6_mc2: 295008 B of shared memory (6 stages x 49152 B + 96 B of barriers) exceeds the 232448 B per-CTA ceiling
      why Depth 6 at 128x256. Expected to decline by a wider margin, which pins the ceiling rather than leaving it at one data point
  w3c_s6_off   wgmma_nt_f16_128x128x64_s6          128x128x64 s6 cluster 1x1x1 mc -, 384 thr, 32p/168c regs | 6 stages x 32768 B + 96 B barriers = 196704 B of 232448 B (84.6%)
      why D1's moderate-size arm (128x128x64 s6), which round 1 excluded because a second kernel in ITS contender arm would have been an A/B against two things at once. Here every row is scored against the same cuBLAS, so it is a row
  w3c_s6_mcb2  wgmma_nt_f16_128x128x64_s6_mcb2     128x128x64 s6 cluster 1x2x1 mc B, 384 thr, 32p/168c regs | 6 stages x 32768 B + 96 B barriers = 196704 B of 232448 B (84.6%)
      why The SQUARE tile with the B cluster. At bm == bn the two axes have IDENTICAL I_cta (85.33 either way), so this row and w3c_s6_mc2 are the round's control on the claim that the axis matters only through the traffic arithmetic: a difference between them is mechanism (raster locality, multicast issue cost), not roof
  w3c_s6_mc2   wgmma_nt_f16_128x128x64_s6_mc2      128x128x64 s6 cluster 2x1x1 mc A, 384 thr, 32p/168c regs | 6 stages x 32768 B + 96 B barriers = 196704 B of 232448 B (84.6%)
      why The square tile with the A cluster -- the other half of that control pair, and round 2's 56.9% at sq4096 against the un-clustered 57.5%
  -> 12 of 14 rows are launchable on this part (232448 B carveout). A decline is a finding: clustering does
     not change the ring's size, so the depth axis stops at the same place at BOTH cluster settings.

---- wgmma_config_sweep: correctness, before a single timed launch ----
Every row runs at M = 2 x its own BM AND N = 2 x its own BN -- two tiles on BOTH grid axes -- because the
two clustered arms sit on DIFFERENT axes: a 2x1x1 cluster spans two CTAs along x and a 1x2x1 spans two
along y, and a shape that is one tile wide on an arm's own axis makes its multicast a copy to self, which
proves nothing. A multicast defect is silently wrong data on half the accumulator rows (A arm) or half
its columns (B arm), so the verdict is `==` against an f64 reference over exact-integer operands, never a
tolerance. A fast wrong kernel must not post a timing.
